.TH TKGALLERY 1
.SH NAME
tkgallery \- render one of every widget, in both styles, to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tkgallery
[
.B -f
.I font
]
[
.B -o
.I prefix
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tkgallery
builds one of every widget
.I libtk
has \(em label, button, checkbutton with and without its indicator,
radiobutton, menubutton,
.IR choicebutton (9),
entry, scale, scrollbar, listbox, text, a row of frames showing each
relief, and the
.IR tknotebook (2)
and
.IR tkspinbox (2)
compositions \(em renders them once under each of
.IR style (9)'s
two looks, and writes the result as two PPM files,
.IB prefix -classic.ppm
and
.IB prefix -flat.ppm .
The default prefix is
.BR gallery .
.PP
.B -f
sets the font for the toplevel, which is also what decides the border
width under the flat style.
.PP
It exists because the one item in the widget work that cannot be settled
by measurement is what the widgets look like, and that has to be looked
at by the person whose taste it is.
.IR Tkofftest (1)
can already say whether a flat border uses fewer distinct values than a
chisel; it cannot say which is nicer.
.SS How it can draw at all
.I Image.readpixels
returns
.B "00 00 00"
for a window on the display's screen and for
.I display.image
itself under the Metal present path, so what a widget puts on the page is
invisible to the program that drew it.
A Tk toplevel does not have to be given the display's screen, though:
allocate a
.I Screen
over an ordinary image, make a window in it, and hand the toplevel that
window under the request id
.IR tkclient (2)
uses when there is no window manager,
.BR -1 .
Tk then draws into memory, where
.I readpixels
works.
.IR Tkofftest (1)
uses the same trick to measure widgets; this one uses it to see them.
.PP
PPM because PPM is a header and the bytes, and needs nothing from the
tree.
Anything on the far side can turn it into a PNG.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/sh.dis \-c 'tkgallery \-o /tmp/g'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tkgallery.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR style (9),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR tknotebook (2),
.IR tkspinbox (2)
.SH BUGS
It needs a window system to allocate a display, so it runs under
.I emu\-cocoa
and not
.IR emu\-g ,
even though it never puts anything on the screen.
.PP
The sizes in it are distances, not character cells.
This Tk's
.B -width
on an entry or a listbox counts pixels where real Tk counts characters,
so
.B "-width 22"
is a field three letters wide and
.B "-height 3"
is a listbox three pixels tall.
Both of those were in the first version of this file and neither is an
error any command reports.
.PP
Nothing here is checked.
A widget that stopped drawing entirely would produce a smaller picture
and no complaint;
.IR tkofftest (1)
is what asserts, and this is what shows.
